{
 "id": "Matrices-Inverses-Definition",
 "title_en": "Definition and uniqueness",
 "blocks": [
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "We now define a matrix operation which in some ways plays the role of division. We cannot divide by a matrix, but we can multiply by the inverse of a matrix, which is almost as good.",
    "sha": "9d77eb1d0b00",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "definition",
   "title": {
    "en": "The inverse of a matrix",
    "sha": "fafc1d51394a",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Let $A$ and $B$ be $n\\times n$-matrices. We say that $B$ is an **inverse** of $A$ if",
      "sha": "465f87e4c021",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "BA=I\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n    AB=I."
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "If this is the case, we also write $B=A^{-1}$. When a matrix has an inverse, it is called **invertible**.",
      "sha": "a3c3a92ea164",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "example",
   "title": {
    "en": "Verifying the inverse of a matrix",
    "sha": "4d551b130615",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Let $A=\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{array}\\right]$. Check that $B=\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\end{array}\\right]$ is an inverse of $A$.",
      "sha": "ec9a25f950a0",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "solution",
   "title": null,
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "To check this, multiply",
      "sha": "e5c909aad697",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "AB = \\left[\\begin{array}{rr}\n      1 & 1 \\\\\n      1 & 2\n    \\end{array}\\right] \\left[\\begin{array}{rr}\n      2 & -1 \\\\\n      -1 & 1\n    \\end{array}\\right] =\\allowbreak \\left[\\begin{array}{rr}\n      1 & 0 \\\\\n      0 & 1\n    \\end{array}\\right] = I"
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "and",
      "sha": "cffa50a32cb1",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "BA = \\left[\\begin{array}{rr}\n      2 & -1 \\\\\n      -1 & 1\n    \\end{array}\\right] \\left[\\begin{array}{rr}\n      1 & 1 \\\\\n      1 & 2\n    \\end{array}\\right] =\\allowbreak \\left[\\begin{array}{rr}\n      1 & 0 \\\\\n      0 & 1\n    \\end{array}\\right] = I."
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "This shows that $B$ is indeed an inverse of $A$.",
      "sha": "9fc35e0c0497",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "Unlike multiplication of scalars, it can happen that $A\\neq 0$ but $A$ does not have an inverse. This is illustrated in the following example.",
    "sha": "6acfb5dcbedd",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "example",
   "title": {
    "en": "A non-zero matrix with no inverse",
    "sha": "f9020dd033ed",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Let $A=\\left[\\begin{array}{rr} 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{array}\\right]$. Show that $A$ is not invertible.",
      "sha": "19277a54a35e",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "solution",
   "title": null,
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "One might think $A$ has an inverse because it does not equal zero. However, note that",
      "sha": "84de24f19edf",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\left[\\begin{array}{rr}\n      1 & 1 \\\\\n      1 & 1\n    \\end{array}\\right] \\left[\\begin{array}{r}\n      -1 \\\\\n      1\n    \\end{array}\\right] =\\left[\\begin{array}{r}\n      0 \\\\\n      0\n    \\end{array}\\right]."
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "If an inverse $A^{-1}$ existed, we would have the following:",
      "sha": "8fb0ad623616",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\begin{array}{rcl}\\left[\\begin{array}{r}\n      -1 \\\\\n      1\n    \\end{array}\\right]\n    &=&\n        I\\left[\\begin{array}{r}\n          -1 \\\\\n          1\n        \\end{array}\\right] \\\\\n    &=&\n        (A^{-1}A) \\left[\\begin{array}{r}\n          -1 \\\\\n          1\n        \\end{array}\\right] \\\\\n    &=&\n        A^{-1}\\paren{A\\left[\\begin{array}{r}\n            -1 \\\\\n            1\n          \\end{array}\\right]} \\\\\n    &=&\n        A^{-1}\\paren{\\left[\\begin{array}{r}\n            0 \\\\\n            0\n          \\end{array}\\right]} \\\\\n    &=&\n        \\left[\\begin{array}{r}\n          0 \\\\\n          0\n        \\end{array}\\right].\\end{array}"
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "This says that",
      "sha": "16daf8405b64",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "\\left[\\begin{array}{r}\n      -1 \\\\\n      1\n    \\end{array}\\right]\n    =\n    \\left[\\begin{array}{r}\n      0 \\\\\n      0\n    \\end{array}\\right],"
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "which is impossible! Therefore, $A$ does not have an inverse.",
      "sha": "3f273432d626",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "p",
   "t": {
    "en": "Can a matrix have more than one inverse? It turns out that this is not the case: the following theorem shows that if $A$ has an inverse, then the inverse is unique. We can therefore speak of “the” inverse, rather than just “an” inverse, of $A$.",
    "sha": "0e103154f31f",
    "zh": ""
   }
  },
  {
   "k": "box",
   "env": "theorem",
   "title": {
    "en": "Uniqueness of inverse",
    "sha": "d9e882651134",
    "zh": ""
   },
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Suppose $A$ is an $n\\times n$-matrix such that both $B$ and $C$ are inverses of $A$. Then $B=C$.",
      "sha": "f097d36e1aa2",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "k": "proof",
   "title": null,
   "blocks": [
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "By assumption, both $B$ and $C$ are inverses of $A$, so we have $AB=I$, $BA=I$, $AC=I$, and $CA=I$. Using the associative and unit properties of matrix multiplication, we have:",
      "sha": "3c1f69cacddd",
      "zh": ""
     }
    },
    {
     "k": "math",
     "tex": "B = BI = B(AC) = (BA)C = IC = C."
    },
    {
     "k": "p",
     "t": {
      "en": "Therefore, $B=C$, as desired.",
      "sha": "e67facb0727f",
      "zh": ""
     }
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "exercises": [],
 "title_zh": "定义与唯一性"
}
